# Security Rules — suparship

## Secrets Handling

### Golden Rule

> **NEVER commit secret values to Git. Only commit references.**

### Reference Format

```yaml
# CORRECT: Reference format
env:
  DATABASE_URL: ref:db-secret.url
  API_KEY: ref:api-credentials.key

# WRONG: Literal secret value
env:
  DATABASE_URL: "postgresql://user:password@host/db"  # PROHIBITED
```

### Reference Resolution

References are resolved at runtime by the target cluster, not by suparship:

```yaml
# Rendered output (committed to Git)
env:
  - name: DATABASE_URL
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: db-secret
        key: url
```

### Secret Detection in CI

CI MUST run secret scanning:

```bash
# .github/workflows/ci.yaml
- name: Scan for secrets
  uses: trufflesecurity/trufflehog@main
  with:
    path: ./
    fail: true
```

### Files That MUST NOT Contain Secrets

- `*.yaml`, `*.yml` in gitops-output/
- `*.yaml`, `*.yml` in templates/
- `*.go` source files
- `.env` files (must be in `.gitignore`)
- `values.yaml` files

## Demo Secret Policy

### Demo-Only Exceptions

For `--profile=demo`, dummy credentials are acceptable with clear marking:

```yaml
# ONLY for demo profile
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: demo-credentials
  labels:
    suparship.io/demo-only: "true"  # REQUIRED label
  annotations:
    suparship.io/warning: "DEMO ONLY - Do not use in production"
stringData:
  username: demo-user
  password: demo-password-not-for-production
```

### Demo Secret Rules

1. MUST have label `suparship.io/demo-only: "true"`
2. MUST have warning annotation
3. MUST NOT be generated for `core` or `full` profiles
4. Values MUST be obviously fake (no realistic passwords)

## Supply Chain Security

### No Remote Script Execution

```go
// PROHIBITED
exec.Command("bash", "-c", "curl https://example.com/install.sh | bash")

// PROHIBITED in generated output
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/...
```

### Allowed Remote Resources

Remote resources are allowed ONLY with verification:

```go
// REQUIRED: Checksum verification for downloads
func downloadWithChecksum(url, expectedSHA256 string) ([]byte, error) {
    data, err := download(url)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    
    actualHash := sha256.Sum256(data)
    if hex.EncodeToString(actualHash[:]) != expectedSHA256 {
        return nil, fmt.Errorf("checksum mismatch for %s", url)
    }
    return data, nil
}
```

### Helm Chart Pinning

```go
// internal/versions/charts.go
var HelmCharts = map[string]ChartVersion{
    "argocd": {
        Repo:    "https://argoproj.github.io/argo-helm",
        Chart:   "argo-cd",
        Version: "5.51.6",
        AppVersion: "v2.9.3",
    },
    "kargo": {
        Repo:    "https://charts.kargo.io",
        Chart:   "kargo",
        Version: "0.4.0",
        AppVersion: "v0.4.0",
    },
}
```

### Container Image Policy

```yaml
# REQUIRED: Specific tag or digest
image: ghcr.io/argoproj/argocd:v2.9.3

# BETTER: Digest for immutability
image: ghcr.io/argoproj/argocd@sha256:abc123...

# PROHIBITED: Latest or mutable tags in non-demo
image: ghcr.io/argoproj/argocd:latest
image: ghcr.io/argoproj/argocd:stable
```

## RBAC Policy

### Least Privilege Principle

suparship-created ServiceAccounts MUST have minimal permissions:

```yaml
# GOOD: Specific permissions
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: suparship-deployer
  namespace: env-dev
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "patch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["services", "configmaps"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "patch"]

# PROHIBITED: Cluster admin or wildcards
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["*"]
    resources: ["*"]
    verbs: ["*"]
```

### Namespace-Scoped by Default

- Use `Role` + `RoleBinding` (namespace-scoped) by default
- Use `ClusterRole` + `ClusterRoleBinding` only when required
- Document why cluster-level access is needed

### ArgoCD RBAC

ArgoCD Application access SHOULD be project-scoped:

```yaml
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: AppProject
metadata:
  name: myproject
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  destinations:
    - namespace: env-*
      server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
  sourceRepos:
    - https://github.com/org/gitops
  # Deny cluster-scoped resources by default
  clusterResourceWhitelist: []
  namespaceResourceWhitelist:
    - group: '*'
      kind: '*'
```

## Prohibited Patterns

### In Production Code Paths

```go
// PROHIBITED: Remote apply
kubectl apply -f https://...

// PROHIBITED: Unverified exec
exec.Command("bash", "-c", userInput)

// PROHIBITED: Hardcoded credentials
const apiKey = "sk-..."

// PROHIBITED: Disabled TLS verification
client := &http.Client{
    Transport: &http.Transport{
        TLSClientConfig: &tls.Config{InsecureSkipVerify: true}, // NO
    },
}
```

### In Templates

```yaml
# PROHIBITED: Privileged containers (except where required)
securityContext:
  privileged: true

# PROHIBITED: Host networking (except ingress controller)
hostNetwork: true

# PROHIBITED: Running as root without justification
securityContext:
  runAsUser: 0
```

## Audit Requirements

### What Must Be Auditable

1. All changes to cluster state (via Git commits)
2. Who approved changes (via PR approvals)
3. What was deployed (via ArgoCD sync history)
4. When changes happened (via Git timestamps)

### Git Commit Signing (Recommended)

```bash
# For production deployments
git config commit.gpgsign true
```

## Vulnerability Scanning

### Required Scans

| Tool | Purpose | When |
|------|---------|------|
| `trufflehog` | Secret detection | Every PR |
| `trivy` | Container CVE scan | Before release |
| `gosec` | Go security lint | Every PR |
| `helm lint` | Chart validation | Every PR |

### Example CI Integration

```yaml
security-scan:
  runs-on: ubuntu-latest
  steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4
    
    - name: Go security scan
      run: gosec ./...
    
    - name: Secret scan
      uses: trufflesecurity/trufflehog@main
    
    - name: Container scan
      uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
      with:
        scan-type: fs
        scan-ref: .
```

## Incident Response

### If Secrets Are Committed

1. **Immediately** rotate the exposed credential
2. Force-push to remove from Git history (or use BFG)
3. Notify affected parties
4. Add to `.gitignore` and scanning rules
5. Post-incident review

### Credential Rotation Checklist

- [ ] Identify all locations where credential is used
- [ ] Generate new credential
- [ ] Update in secret management system
- [ ] Verify services restart with new credential
- [ ] Invalidate old credential
- [ ] Document in incident log
